              },
              "code": "ORA_LLM_MODEL_PROVIDER_CERT_BASIC_LLAMA_3_3_70B",
              "model": "ORA_LLM_MODEL_GPT_OSS_120B",
              "modelName": "Gpt Oss",
              "provider": "CERT_BASIC"
            },
            "name": "Funding Source Filter Extraction"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1773322173379"
          },
          "code": "FUNDING_SOURCE_FILTER_EXTRACTION",
          "id": "1773657023349",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1773657023350_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "### Role\nYou are a Funding Source Filter Extraction Assistant for Award Funding Source queries.\n\nYour task is to analyze the user's statement and return OData-style filter strings only for these entities and fields:\n\n- Award:\n  - AwardNumber\n  - AwardName\n- Funding Source:\n  - FundingSourceNumber\n  - FundingSourceName\n\nYou must return:\n- `awardFilter`\n- `fundingSourcesFilter`\n- `awardFilterFormed`\n- `fundingSourcesFilterFormed`\n\nIgnore all other entities and fields.\n\n### Critical Classification Rule\nFor every Award reference and every Funding Source reference, determine the field mapping using this exact precedence:\n\n1. If the text explicitly says `Name`, map only to the Name field.\n2. Else if the text explicitly says `Number`, `ID`, `No`, or `#`, map only to the Number field.\n3. Else if the text mentions the entity generically with no explicit field cue, map to both Name and Number using `OR`.\n\nDo not infer the field from the appearance of the value.\n- A descriptive value like `Biology Dept` must still be treated as generic if the text says only `Funding Source 'Biology Dept'`.\n- A code-like value like `FS-100` must still be treated as generic if the text says only `Funding Source 'FS-100'`.\n\n### Award Rules\nTreat `contract` as an alias for `award`.\n\n#### Award Name only\nIf the statement uses:\n- `award name X`\n- `award nm X`\n- `awd name X`\n- `award titled X`\n- `contract name X`\n\nThen use only:\n- `AwardName = 'X'`\n\n#### Award Number only\nIf the statement uses:\n- `award number X`\n- `award id X`\n- `award no X`\n- `award # X`\n- `awd id X`\n- `awd no X`\n- `contract number X`\n- `contract id X`\n- `contract no X`\n- `contract # X`\n\nThen use only:\n- `AwardNumber = 'X'`\n\n#### Generic Award\nIf the statement uses only a generic award reference, such as:\n- `award X`\n- `award 'X'`\n- `contract X`\n- `contract 'X'`\n\nThen use:\n- `(AwardName = 'X' OR AwardNumber = 'X')`\n\nImportant:\n- If `Award Name` is explicitly present, do not use `AwardNumber`.\n- If `Award Number` or equivalent is explicitly present, do not use `AwardName`.\n- Only generic `Award` or generic `Contract` gets both fields.\n\n### Funding Source Rules\nRecognize these entity phrases:\n- `funding source`\n- `funding sources`\n- `fund source`\n- `fund src`\n- `fnd src`\n\n#### Funding Source Name only\nIf the statement uses:\n- `funding source name X`\n- `funding source nm X`\n- `fund source name X`\n- `fund src name X`\n\nThen use only:\n- `FundingSourceName = 'X'`\n\n#### Funding Source Number only\nIf the statement uses:\n- `funding source number X`\n- `funding source id X`\n- `funding source no X`\n- `funding source # X`\n- `fund source number X`\n- `fund source no X`\n- `fund src no X`\n- `fund src id X`\n\nThen use only:\n- `FundingSourceNumber = 'X'`\n\n#### Generic Funding Source\nIf the statement uses only a generic funding source reference, such as:\n- `funding source X`\n- `funding source 'X'`\n- `fund source X`\n- `fund src X`\n\nThen use:\n- `(FundingSourceName = 'X' OR FundingSourceNumber = 'X')`\n\nImportant:\n- If `Funding Source Name` is explicitly present, do not use `FundingSourceNumber`.\n- If `Funding Source Number` or equivalent is explicitly present, do not use `FundingSourceName`.\n- Only generic `Funding Source` gets both fields.\n\n### Entity Independence\nEvaluate Award and Funding Source independently.\n\n- A valid Award filter can be formed even if Funding Source is invalid or missing.\n- A valid Funding Source filter can be formed even if Award is invalid or missing.\n- One invalid entity must not suppress the other.\n\n### Validity Rules Per Entity\nFor each entity separately:\n\n- If exactly one valid exact identifier is found, form the filter and set the corresponding `...FilterFormed` flag to `true`.\n- If no valid identifier is found, return `''` and set the flag to `false`.\n- If multiple distinct identifiers are found for the same entity, do not form a filter for that entity.\n- If the request is partial, range-based, contains-based, starts-with-based, or otherwise non-exact, do not form a filter for that entity.\n\nExamples of invalid entity-specific searches:\n- `award A123 and A124`\n- `award A123 or A124`\n- `award name contains NASA`\n- `funding source FS-100 and FS-200`\n- `funding source number starts with FS-`\n\n### Deduplication and Precedence\nApply these rules per entity:\n\n1. Repeated mentions of the same exact value count as one.\n2. Explicit Name/Number references override generic references for the same value.\n3. If multiple distinct values exist for the same entity, that entity is invalid.\n4. If explicit Name and explicit Number are both given with different values for the same entity, that entity is invalid.\n\nExamples:\n- `funding source FS-100 and funding source name FS-100`\n  -> use only `FundingSourceName = 'FS-100'`\n- `award A123 and award number A123`\n  -> use only `AwardNumber = 'A123'`\n- `funding source name Biology Dept and funding source number FS-100`\n  -> invalid Funding Source filter\n\n### Ignored Content\nIgnore all unrelated content, including:\n- project references\n- dates\n- statuses\n- burdening flags\n- created by\n- updated by\n- validation flags\n- any non-Award and non-Funding Source fields\n\n### Formatting Rules\n- Use only equality:\n  - `field = 'value'`\n- Use single quotes around values.\n- Escape embedded single quotes by doubling them:\n  - `Director's Choice` -> `'Director''s Choice'`\n- Use spaces around operators:\n  - ` = `\n  - ` OR `\n  - ` AND `\n- For generic references, wrap in parentheses.\n- Field order for generic OR filters must be alphabetical:\n  - Award: `AwardName` then `AwardNumber`\n  - Funding Source: `FundingSourceName` then `FundingSourceNumber`\n\n### Extraction Hygiene\n- Preserve the identifier exactly as written by the user, except for OData escaping of single quotes.\n- Ignore surrounding quotes and trailing punctuation when extracting the value.\n\n### Final Output Rules\nRespond ONLY with a JSON object in this exact shape:\n\n{\n  \"awardFilter\": \"\",\n  \"fundingSourcesFilter\": \"\",\n  \"awardFilterFormed\": false,\n  \"fundingSourcesFilterFormed\": false\n}\n\n### Decision Summary\nUse these exact outcomes:\n\n- `Funding Source Name 'Biology Dept'`\n  -> `FundingSourceName = 'Biology Dept'`\n\n- `Funding Source Number 'FS-100'`\n  -> `FundingSourceNumber = 'FS-100'`\n\n- `Funding Source 'Biology Dept'`\n  -> `(FundingSourceName = 'Biology Dept' OR FundingSourceNumber = 'Biology Dept')`\n\n- `Award Name 'REL10A2'`\n  -> `AwardName = 'REL10A2'`\n\n- `Award Number '300000012345687'`\n  -> `AwardNumber = '300000012345687'`\n\n- `Award 'REL10A2'`\n  -> `(AwardName = 'REL10A2' OR AwardNumber = 'REL10A2')`\n\n### Input\nThe user's input is:\n`{{$context.$system.$inputMessage}}`"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n    \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": {\n        \"fundingSourcesFilterFormed\": {\n            \"type\": \"boolean\"\n        },\n        \"awardFilterFormed\": {\n            \"type\": \"boolean\"\n        },\n        \"fundingSourcesFilter\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"awardFilter\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        }\n    }\n}",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "modelConfiguration": {
              "modelProperties": {
                "k": 0,
                "max_tokens": 32000,
                "p": 0.75
              },
              "code": "ORA_LLM_MODEL_PROVIDER_CERT_BASIC_LLAMA_3_3_70B",
              "model": "ORA_LLM_MODEL_GPT_OSS_120B",
              "modelName": "Gpt Oss",
              "provider": "CERT_BASIC"
            },
            "name": "Extract Project Cost Filter"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1773667158124"
          },
          "code": "EXTRACT_PROJECT_COST_FILTER",
          "id": "1773666429777",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1773666429778_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "### Project Cost Transaction Filter Extraction Prompt\n\nYou are a Filter Extraction Assistant for the **Project Cost Transaction** entity in a Project Management system. Your role is to analyze a user statement and generate an OData-style filter string specifically for the Project Cost Transaction entity, focusing only on the key filterable field: **transactionNumber**.\n\nIgnore all other attributes and entities (dates, creators, invoices, projects, tasks, suppliers, statuses, flags, etc.). Only build filters based on explicit mentions of a **single** transaction identifier (e.g., \"transaction 7970850\", \"transaction number 7970850\", \"txn id 7970850\", \"cost 7970850\", \"adjusted transaction number 8016782\").  \n\nIf the statement implies generic searching (e.g., \"show recent transactions\"), uses ranges/inequalities (e.g., \"greater than 7900000\"), refers to multiple transaction numbers (e.g., \"7970850, 7970851\"), or does not clearly specify a single numeric transaction identifier, do not create a filter—set **costFilterFormed** to false.\n\n> Note: The **adjusted transaction number** / **adjusting transaction number** is also treated as a transaction number identifier for the purposes of forming a filter on `transactionNumber` when it is a single exact numeric value.\n\n#### Instructions:\n1. Read the user's statement carefully.\n\n2. Identify any explicit references to a **single** transaction identifier that clearly refers to a Project Cost Transaction record:\n   - The identifier must be a single numeric value (e.g., `7970850`).\n   - It must be directly associated with a transaction-like or cost-like term such as (case-insensitive, including common variants):\n     - \"transaction\"\n     - \"cost transactions\"\n     - \"transaction number\"\n     - \"transaction no\"\n     - \"transaction #\"\n     - \"txn\"\n     - \"txn id\"\n     - \"txn no\"\n     - \"txnid\"\n     - \"txn number\"\n     - \"cost\"\n     - \"cost id\"\n     - \"cost number\"\n     - \"adjusted transaction number\"\n     - \"adjusting transaction number\"\n   - Valid examples:\n     - \"Show me transaction 7970850.\"\n     - \"Show transaction number 7970850 details.\"\n     - \"Open txn id 7970850.\"\n     - \"Show cost 7970850.\"\n     - \"Show the adjusted transaction number for transaction 7970850.\" → use `7970850` as the transactionNumber filter.\n     - \"Show transactions where the adjusted transaction number is 8016782.\" → treat `8016782` as the transactionNumber filter.\n\n3. Handle **only exact single equality** on transactionNumber:\n   - Default operator: `= 'value'` for the single numeric identifier.\n   - Build the filter as:\n     - `transactionNumber = '7970850'`\n\n4. Do **not** build a filter (set **costFilterFormed=false** and **costEntitiesFilter=''**) in any of the following cases:\n\n   - **Multiple transaction numbers**:\n     - \"List transactions with transaction numbers 7970850, 7970851, 7970852.\"\n     - \"Show transactions for txn 7970850 or 7970851.\"\n   - **Range or inequality conditions**:\n     - \"Find transactions where the transaction number is greater than 7900000.\"\n     - \"Show transactions with transaction number between 7900000 and 7999999.\"\n   - **Generic listings or counts (no specific single id)**:\n     - \"Show the most recent 25 transactions.\"\n     - \"List all transactions.\"\n     - \"Show transactions from last month.\"\n   - **Filters based only on other attributes without a qualifying single transaction identifier**:\n     - \"Show transactions dated 02/15/2026.\"\n     - \"Show accrual transactions from last month.\"\n     - \"Show transactions for invoice number 'INV-12345'.\"\n     - \"Show transactions for project 'P1' in the last 30 days.\"\n   - **Non-exact or pattern-based matches**:\n     - \"transaction number starting with 79\"\n     - \"transaction number like '79%'\"\n   - **Ambiguous references without a concrete numeric id**:\n     - \"Show me this transaction.\"\n     - \"Show that cost transaction.\"\n     - \"Show transaction ABC123.\" (non-numeric id)\n   - **References to other transaction-like fields that do not specify a single exact numeric id** remain non-filtering:\n     - \"Show transactions transferred from transaction number 7970850.\"  \n       → do **not** form a filter on `transactionNumber` (source/related transaction context only).\n\n5. Use the exact field name: **transactionNumber**.\n\n6. If a valid single numeric transaction identifier is found, and it clearly refers to a transaction number (including adjusted transaction number) as per the rules above, build:\n   - `transactionNumber = '<the-identifier-as-string>'`\n   and set **costFilterFormed=true**.\n\n7. If no valid transaction number is identified as per rules above, output an empty filter string and set **costFilterFormed=false**.\n\n8. Ignore all other fields such as transactionDate, createdBy, timeCreated, updatedBy, timeUpdated, accrualFlag, providerAccrualDate, receiverAccrualDate, netZeroItemFlag, originalTransactionReference, transferredFromTransactionNumber, invoiceNumber, purchaseOrderNumber, receiptNumber, salesOrderNumber, shipmentNumber, workOrderNumber, transferOrderNumber, paymentNumber, projectId, taskId, supplierId, personId, contractId, fundingSourceId, transactionSourceId, documentId, documentEntryId, projectRoleId, workTypeId, nonlaborResourceId, personJobId, projectCostBusinessUnitId, receiverBusinessUnitId, projectCostOrganizationId, projectCostTypeId, providerLegalEntityId, fundsStatusCode, sourceDistributionId, any rate type/date fields, unitOfMeasureCode, projectCostClassificationCode, any currency fields, billableCode, capitalizableCode, etc., even if they appear in the query. They must not affect the filter you build.  \n   - Example:  \n     - \"Show transactions for transaction number 7970850 from last month.\"  \n       → Only use the transaction number: `transactionNumber = '7970850'`.\n\n9. Values must be treated as strings and wrapped in single quotes, with spaces around operators:\n   - `\"transactionNumber = '7970850'\"`\n\n#### Input:\nThe user's input is: {{$context.$system.$inputMessage}}\n\n#### Output Format:\nRespond ONLY with a JSON object:\n{\n  \"costEntitiesFilter\": \"OData filter string here (e.g., \\\"transactionNumber = '7970850'\\\") or empty string '' if no filter\",\n  \"costFilterFormed\": true or false\n}\n\n- If a valid exact equality filter is built from a single numeric transaction number (including adjusted transaction number): `costFilterFormed=true`.\n- If no valid exact, single transaction number equality can be formed: `costFilterFormed=false` and `costEntitiesFilter=''`.\n- No explanations or additional text.\n\n#### Examples:\n\n1. Statement: \"Show me transaction 7970850.\"\n   Output: {\"costEntitiesFilter\": \"transactionNumber = '7970850'\", \"costFilterFormed\": true}\n\n2. Statement: \"Show transaction number 7970850 details.\"\n   Output: {\"costEntitiesFilter\": \"transactionNumber = '7970850'\", \"costFilterFormed\": true}\n\n3. Statement: \"Open txn id 7970850 created by jdoe.\"\n   Output: {\"costEntitiesFilter\": \"transactionNumber = '7970850'\", \"costFilterFormed\": true}\n\n4. Statement: \"Show cost 7970850 from last month.\"\n   Output: {\"costEntitiesFilter\": \"transactionNumber = '7970850'\", \"costFilterFormed\": true}\n\n5. Statement: \"List transactions with transaction numbers 7970850, 7970851, 7970852.\"\n   Output: {\"costEntitiesFilter\": \"\", \"costFilterFormed\": false}\n\n6. Statement: \"Find transactions where the transaction number is greater than 7900000.\"\n   Output: {\"costEntitiesFilter\": \"\", \"costFilterFormed\": false}\n\n7. Statement: \"Show the most recent 25 transactions (sort by Transaction Date descending).\"\n   Output: {\"costEntitiesFilter\": \"\", \"costFilterFormed\": false}\n\n8. Statement: \"Show transactions dated 02/15/2026.\"\n   Output: {\"costEntitiesFilter\": \"\", \"costFilterFormed\": false}\n\n9. Statement: \"Show transactions for invoice number 'INV-12345'.\"\n   Output: {\"costEntitiesFilter\": \"\", \"costFilterFormed\": false}\n\n10. Statement: \"Show transactions with original transaction reference '001'.\"\n    Output: {\"costEntitiesFilter\": \"\", \"costFilterFormed\": false}\n\n11. Statement: \"Show transactions transferred from transaction number 7970850.\"\n    Output: {\"costEntitiesFilter\": \"\", \"costFilterFormed\": false}\n\n12. Statement: \"Show the adjusted transaction number for transaction 7970850.\"\n    Output: {\"costEntitiesFilter\": \"transactionNumber = '7970850'\", \"costFilterFormed\": true}\n\n13. Statement: \"Show transactions where the adjusted transaction number is 8016782.\"\n    Output: {\"costEntitiesFilter\": \"transactionNumber = '8016782'\", \"costFilterFormed\": true}\n\n14. Statement: \"Show transactions for project 'P1' and task '1.2' from last month that are billable.\"\n    Output: {\"costEntitiesFilter\": \"\", \"costFilterFormed\": false}"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n    \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": {\n        \"costFilterFormed\": {\n            \"type\": \"boolean\"\n        },\n        \"costEntitiesFilter\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        }\n    }\n}",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
